Different birds fly in different ways. Small birds like sparrows flap fast and often. Their short, rounded wings are good for quick dodges among bushes. Swallows have long, pointed wings, built for speed in open air. [[@hummingbirds|Hummingbirds]] beat their wings up to about 80 times a second and can hover, and even fly backwards.

Big birds save energy. [[@eagles-hunt|Eagles]] and India's [[@vultures-india|vultures]] circle on [[@gliders|thermals]], columns of warm air rising from the sun-heated ground, and barely move their wings for hours. The wandering albatross, with wings about 3.5 m across, glides over the Southern Ocean using the wind just above the waves. It can travel hundreds of kilometres in a day with hardly a flap.

{{story:birds-dinosaurs}}

Most watches and wall clocks today use quartz, a mineral found in ordinary sand. When electricity from a battery passes through a tiny quartz crystal shaped like a tuning fork, it vibrates very steadily, 32,768 times every second.

That number is not random. Halve it 15 times and you get exactly 1. A chip inside the watch does that halving and sends one pulse a second to move the hands or change the screen. Quartz watches arrived in 1969 and soon became cheap enough for almost everyone.

{{story:time-measure}}

Nobody knows exactly who made the first spectacles, but we know roughly when. In 1306, a friar named Giordano da Pisa told a crowd in Florence that the art of making eyeglasses had been invented less than twenty years earlier. Early spectacles were two convex lenses in frames of bone, metal or leather, joined at a rivet and perched on the nose. They helped older people read.

Before that, some readers used polished "reading stones" laid on top of a page to magnify the words. Lenses for short sight came later, in the 1400s. Arms that hook over the ears only became common in the 1700s.

{{story:microscopes}}

Gravity speeds up falling objects. Double it, and a dropped glass would hit the floor about 1.4 times faster. A fall from a tree or a wall would be much more dangerous.

Raindrops would fall faster and hit harder. Rivers would rush more quickly downhill.

{{story:gravity-planets}}

जो जानकार किसी कलाकार के काम को गहराई से जानते हैं, उन्हें पारखी कहते हैं। वे देखते हैं कि चित्रकार ब्रश कैसे चलाता था, हाथ और आँखें कैसे बनाता था, और कौन से रंग उसे पसंद थे। किसी अंदाज़ की नकल करने वाला जालसाज़ अक्सर धीरे-धीरे और बहुत सँभलकर काम करता है, और यही सावधानी अकड़ी, अटकती लकीरों में दिख जाती है।

दस्तख़त नकली बनाना सबसे आसान है, इसलिए जानकार उन पर सबसे कम भरोसा करते हैं। कुछ शोधकर्ता अब कंप्यूटर से ब्रश-स्ट्रोक की लंबाई, घुमाव और मोटाई नापते हैं और उनकी तुलना वैसे ही करते हैं जैसे जासूस [[@fingerprints|उँगलियों के निशान]] मिलाते हैं।

{{story:money-worth}}

The opening lines say India is a sovereign, socialist, secular, democratic [[republic|republic]], and promise justice, liberty, equality and fraternity to all its citizens. Fraternity means a feeling of brotherhood and sisterhood.

The words "socialist" and "secular" were added in 1976, by the 42nd [[amendment|Amendment]]. Many schools across India read the Preamble aloud at assembly.

{{story:ambedkar}}

A painful chapter of migration began in 1834. After slavery was abolished in the British Empire, plantation owners needed new workers. Over the next 80 years or so, more than a million Indians signed contracts called indenture. They sailed to Mauritius, Fiji, Trinidad, Guyana, [[@south-africa|South Africa]] and other places to work on sugar plantations. Conditions were often harsh, and many could never afford to return.

Almost half a million of them first landed at Aapravasi Ghat in Port Louis, Mauritius, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Their [[descendant|descendants]] make up a large share of the people in several of those countries today. In South Africa, a young lawyer named [[@gandhi|Mohandas Gandhi]] first began his fight against unfair treatment of Indians.

{{story:gandhi}}

Picture an ant walking along the middle of a paper loop. On a normal loop, it stays on the outside forever. On a Möbius strip, after one lap it finds itself on what looked like the underside. After two laps, it is back where it started.

The Dutch artist M. C. Escher drew exactly this in 1963. His print *Möbius Strip II* shows nine red ants marching round and round a Möbius strip, never reaching an edge. You can find more of his mind-bending pictures in [[@maths-art-escher|Escher's maths art]].

{{story:maths-art-escher}}

The American mathematician Raymond Smullyan was also a magician and a pianist. He wrote whole books of puzzles about an island of knights, who always tell the truth, and knaves, who always lie.

Suppose two people stand at a fork in the road. One is a knight and one is a knave, but you don't know which. Only one road leads to the village. You may ask one question. The famous trick is to ask: "If I asked the other person which way is the village, what would they say?" Then go the other way. Whether you asked a knight or a knave, the answer you hear is always wrong.

{{story:magic-tricks}}

Ice cream sales and sunburn both go up in summer. That doesn't mean ice cream burns your skin. Hot, sunny weather drives both.

When two things change together, statisticians say they are correlated. That is different from one causing the other. A famous joke chart shows that, in one country, the number of storks and the number of babies born rose together for years. Storks don't bring babies. Both were linked to other changes in the country.

{{story:news-media}}

Serena won her first Grand Slam singles title at the 1999 US Open, aged 17. She was the first Black woman to win a Grand Slam singles title since Althea Gibson in 1958.

The sisters often met each other in finals, which was hard for both. Between 2002 and 2003 Serena won four Grand Slams in a row, beating Venus in all four finals. Fans called it the "Serena Slam". She did it again in 2014–15.

{{story:sports-science}}

In Britain, rubber-soled canvas shoes were called plimsolls. In the USA, they got a different name. Because the soles made almost no noise, people said you could sneak about in them, and they became "sneakers". In 1917, two famous canvas shoes went on sale in the USA: Keds and the Converse All Star.

The Converse All Star was made for the new sport of [[@basketball|basketball]]. A player and salesman named Chuck Taylor travelled the country promoting it, and his name was later printed on the ankle patch.

{{story:friction}}

In the early 1900s, circus strongmen and acrobats performed in tight, stretchy costumes that showed off their muscles. Many wore belts and boots, and some wore short trunks over their tights, just like Superman. Readers already linked this look with strength and daring.

Capes, meanwhile, were the clothes of heroes in stage plays and adventure films, such as Zorro and Dracula. They suggested someone dramatic and a little bit royal. Kings and queens had worn long cloaks for centuries, so a cape quietly said: this person is important.

{{story:airplanes-fly}}

Lifespans in nature vary hugely. A mayfly may live only a day as an adult. A mouse lives two or three years. But [[@extreme-longest-living|some animals age very slowly]]. Bowhead whales can live over 200 years. In 2016, scientists studying the eyes of Greenland sharks estimated that one of them may have been around 400 years old. That estimate could be off by more than a century either way.

One tiny jellyfish, *Turritopsis dohrnii*, can turn itself back into a young stage when it is hurt or stressed. It is sometimes called the immortal jellyfish, which you can read about in [[@jellyfish-old|jellyfish that never die]].

{{story:jellyfish-old}}

तिल, जिसे डॉक्टर नीवस कहते हैं, तब बनता है जब मेलानोसाइट एक-सा फैलने के बजाय एक जगह झुंड बनाकर इकट्ठा हो जाते हैं। ज़्यादातर तिल बचपन और किशोर उम्र में उभरते हैं, और कुछ लगभग 40 की उम्र तक उभरते रहते हैं। कुछ गिने-चुने बच्चे जन्म से ही एक तिल लेकर आते हैं।

एक आम बड़े व्यक्ति के 10 से 40 के बीच तिल होते हैं। ज़िंदगी भर में तिल धीरे-धीरे बदल सकते हैं। कुछ हल्के या उभरे हुए हो जाते हैं, और बहुत-से बुढ़ापे में मिट जाते हैं। लगता है कि बचपन में खूब धूप लगने से इंसान के तिलों की गिनती बढ़ जाती है।

{{story:dna}}

Before people understood germs, dirty water spread diseases like cholera in cities everywhere. In 1854, a London doctor named John Snow traced one cholera outbreak to a single water pump. His work helped show that diseases could travel in water, and cities began building proper water systems and [[@toilets-sewers|sewers]].

Today, according to the WHO and UNICEF, about 2.1 billion people still lacked safely managed drinking water at home in 2024, about one in four people on Earth. Clean water is one of the [[@sustainable-goals|Sustainable Development Goals]] the world has set for 2030.

{{story:disease-history}}

In 1928, a British commission arrived in India to discuss reforms, with no Indian members. Protesters in Lahore marched against it, led by Lala Lajpat Rai. Police beat him with lathis, and he died a few weeks later.

Bhagat Singh's group, the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association, decided to take revenge on the police superintendent they blamed. On 17 December 1928, they shot a young officer named John Saunders by mistake. Bhagat Singh escaped Lahore in disguise, wearing Western clothes and a hat, and with his hair cut.

{{story:human-rights}}

The Portuguese did not just trade. They used cannons on their ships to force their way. Afonso de Albuquerque, a Portuguese commander, seized Goa in 1510 and made it the capital of Portuguese Asia. He also captured Hormuz in the Persian Gulf and Malacca in Southeast Asia, to control key sea lanes.

At sea, they made Indian and Arab ships buy a pass, called a cartaz, to sail safely. Ships without one could be attacked. Local rulers such as the Zamorins of Calicut, whose admirals the Kunjali Marakkars fought the Portuguese at sea for nearly a century, resisted fiercely.

{{story:spices}}

By 1860, California was booming after the [[@gold-rush|gold rush]], and more than 300,000 people lived there. Yet it was cut off from the eastern states by deserts, mountains and plains with no railway. The country was also sliding towards civil war, and Californians wanted fast news.

Three businessmen, William Russell, Alexander Majors and William Waddell, decided to prove that a fast central route could work. They hoped it would win them a valuable government mail contract. They bought around 400 horses and built a chain of relay stations across the West in just a few months.

{{story:telegraph}}

The old bridge had a clever middle. Two steel leaves, each weighing about 415 tonnes, tilted up to open a gap for ships. It was called a Scherzer rolling lift. Workers opened it by hand, with [[lever|levers]], and each leaf took about 45 minutes.

The sea was hard on the bridge. Salt spray causes [[@rust|rust]], and the Pamban area is one of the most corrosive places for steel in India. Engineers painted, patched and replaced parts for a century. In December 2022, the old lifting span was found too weak, and trains stopped for good.

{{story:bridges}}

Sugar loves to form [[@gems-crystals|crystals]]. Whether candy ends up crystal or glass depends on how it cools.

Rock sugar, called mishri in India, is made of big crystals grown slowly from syrup over days. Fudge and many Indian burfis are stirred while they cool, which makes millions of tiny crystals, so the texture is smooth and crumbly. For hard candy, makers want no crystals at all. They boil the syrup very hot, add a little glucose syrup or [[acid|acid]] to block crystals, and cool it fast. The sugar freezes in a jumble, like glass, and that is why a clear lollipop lets light through.

{{story:gems-crystals}}

Cashews belong to the same plant family as [[@mango-tree|mangoes]], pistachios and poison ivy. Many plants in this family contain irritating oils.

The cashew shell is filled with a dark, sticky oil that can burn the skin and cause painful rashes. That's why cashews are never sold in their shells. Every nut is roasted or steamed to make the oil safer before the shell is cracked open. Mango skin contains a related substance, which is why a few people get an itchy rash around the mouth from [[@mango-varieties|eating mangoes]].

{{story:mango-tree}}

In Japanese schools, lunch, called *kyushoku*, is part of the timetable. There's usually no canteen. Children take turns putting on white coats, caps and masks, then wheel trolleys of food to their classroom and serve everyone. After eating together, they clear up and sort the rubbish.

Meals are planned by trained nutritionists and are often cooked from fresh local food. A typical tray might hold rice, a bowl of soup, grilled fish and a small salad, with a carton of milk. The custom dates to 1889, when a school in the town of Tsuruoka gave rice balls and salted fish to poor pupils.

{{story:japan-culture}}

India's food map follows its rain. The [[@monsoon|monsoon]] drops heavy rain on the east, the northeast and the west coast. Rice needs plenty of water, so these places became rice country. Bengal, Odisha, Assam, Kerala and Tamil Nadu all eat rice at most meals.

{{story:monsoon}}

The northwest gets less rain but has big rivers and cool winters, which suit wheat. Punjab and Haryana became India's main wheat lands, and rotis and parathas are the everyday food. In Rajasthan and parts of the Deccan, rain is scarce, so tough grains like bajra and jowar were common. Bajra rotis in Rajasthan and jowar bhakri in Maharashtra are still popular.
